Step 4: Render and compare environments
helm template webapp ./webapp-chart --set environment=development | grep -A2 "DEBUG"
helm template webapp ./webapp-chart --set environment=production | grep -A2 "DEBUG"
helm template webapp ./webapp-chart --show-only templates/service.yaml
Deliverable for Task 2: The development render shows DEBUG: "true", the production render shows DEBUG: "false" with larger resource limits, and the service template lists both the http (port 80) and metrics (port 9090) ports produced by the range loop.

Task 3: Deploy, Verify, and Troubleshoot the Release
Step 1: Lint and dry-run validate before installing
helm lint ./webapp-chart
helm template webapp ./webapp-chart | kubectl apply --dry-run=client -f -
Expected result: lint reports no failures, and the dry-run apply reports each resource as created (dry run) with no schema errors.

Step 2: Install the chart onto the kind cluster
helm install webapp ./webapp-chart --set environment=production

kubectl get pods
kubectl get services
kubectl get configmaps
Expected result: helm install prints STATUS: deployed. kubectl get pods shows one pod for the webapp-chart deployment. It typically starts in ContainerCreating for a few seconds while the nginx image is pulled, then moves to Running with READY 1/1.

Step 3: Verify readiness and troubleshoot if needed
Wait for the pod to become ready, then confirm:

kubectl rollout status deployment/webapp-webapp-chart --timeout=60s
kubectl get pods -o wide
If the pod does not reach Running/1/1 within a reasonable time, use these commands to diagnose the problem before re-checking:

# Shows recent events (e.g. ImagePullBackOff, FailedScheduling, readiness probe failures)
kubectl describe pod <pod-name>

# Shows application/container stdout and stderr, useful for CrashLoopBackOff
kubectl logs <pod-name>

# If the container has restarted, view the previous crash's logs
kubectl logs <pod-name> --previous
Common causes and what to look for:

ImagePullBackOff or ErrImagePull in kubectl describe pod events: the image name/tag in values.yaml is wrong or unreachable â verify image.repository and image.tag.
CrashLoopBackOff: check kubectl logs <pod-name> for an application startup error, and confirm the containerPort matches what the process actually listens on.
Pod stuck in Pending: run kubectl describe pod <pod-name> and check the Events section for scheduling failures (for example, insufficient CPU/memory on the single-node kind cluster) â lower the resources.requests values in values.yaml if needed.
Step 4: Upgrade the release and confirm the change took effect
helm upgrade webapp ./webapp-chart --set replicaCount=2 --set environment=staging

kubectl get pods
kubectl get deployment webapp-webapp-chart -o jsonpath='{.spec.replicas}{"\n"}'
helm get values webapp
Expected result: two pods are now listed for the deployment, the jsonpath command prints 2, and helm get values webapp shows replicaCount: 2 and environment: staging as the effective user-supplied values for the release.

Deliverable for Task 3: A running release named webapp with 2 replicas, confirmed through kubectl get pods, kubectl rollout status, and helm get values, along with a demonstrated understanding of kubectl describe pod and kubectl logs as first-line troubleshooting tools.

Expected Outcomes
A working Helm chart (webapp-chart) with templated Deployment, Service, and ConfigMap resources parameterized entirely through values.yaml
Demonstrated use of Helm conditionals (if/else/eq) and loops (range) to produce environment-specific and multi-port Kubernetes manifests
A successfully installed and upgraded Helm release on a local kind cluster, verified with kubectl and helm get values
Troubleshooting
Symptom: helm template or helm install fails with a YAML/parse error mentioning a specific line and an unexpected {{ or indentation problem. Diagnostic hint: Run helm template webapp ./webapp-chart --show-only templates/<file>.yaml in isolation to see the exact rendered output around the failing line, and check that every {{- if }} / {{- range }} has a matching {{- end }}.

Symptom: kubectl get pods shows the pod stuck in Pending or ContainerCreating far longer than expected. Diagnostic hint: Run kubectl describe pod <pod-name> and read the Events section at the bottom for the specific scheduling or image-pull reason before making further changes.

Conclusion
In this lab you built a Helm chart from scratch, extended its default templates with ConfigMaps and conditional logic, and used range loops to generate repeated Kubernetes constructs such as multiple service ports from a single values list. You practiced the full Helm workflow â lint, template, dry-run apply, install, and upgrade â against a real local kind cluster, and learned to verify a release's health and diagnose common failure states with kubectl describe pod, kubectl logs, and helm get values. These skills, chart parameterization, conditional rendering, and systematic troubleshooting, are the core practices needed to build and maintain production-grade Helm charts safely.

Before ending your session, clean up the resources created in this lab:

helm uninstall webapp
kind delete cluster --name helm-lab
To continue building on these skills, explore the official Helm documentation on chart hooks and library charts, and practice writing unit tests for templates using the helm-unittest plugin.
